<chapter xml:id="ch09-the-fundamental-theorem-of-calculus" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Fundamental Theorem of Calculus</title>

  <introduction>
    <p><xref ref="ch08-the-integral-as-accumulation"/> built the integral as accumulated signed change:</p>
    <md>\int_a^b f(x)\,dx.</md>
    <p>At first this integral was a number. It accumulated from one fixed endpoint to another.</p>
    <p>Now we let one endpoint move.</p>
    <p>That small change turns the integral into a function. The surprise is that this new function has a derivative, and its derivative is the function we started with.</p>
    <md>\text{accumulate } f \quad\Longrightarrow\quad \text{differentiate the accumulation} \quad\Longrightarrow\quad f.</md>
    <p>This is the first half of the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus.</p>
  </introduction>

